fields.description:
roll-dih8.11 met the 150 MB budget of docs/RUST-PORT.md Appendix A for replaying 200,000 events on the heap: `cargo run --release --package xtask -- timing --replay 200000 [--ownership]` counts at most 107 MB held at once on the plain history and 133 MB on the ownership-heavy one (2026-10-12, this Mac). The resident peak `/usr/bin/time` reports for the same process is about 185 and 210 MB. The system allocator keeps most of what replay frees (each event's operation once applied, the records the journal no longer needs) for sizes the state it builds never asks for again, so the resident set is about the journal at load plus everything replay allocates. The user's decision of 2026-10-12 (roll-dih8.11) made the heap the budget and sent this gap here.

Ways to close it, none tried:
- free the journal's memory in whole blocks: its records built in an arena per segment, which goes once replay has taken what it needs;
- hold less at once: apply events as they are read instead of loading the journal first (replay needs the causal order first); the local projection (roll-lqjj.2) makes a whole-journal replay the exception rather than every command's cost;
- another allocator that returns freed memory sooner (mimalloc), a new dependency with C code.
